sections.technical:
Blob versioning changes the economics of overwrite-heavy workloads. A delete or overwrite no longer necessarily releases all data. Previous versions remain and continue to incur storage charges until they are explicitly deleted. Microsoft also warns that a large number of versions per blob can increase listing latency and recommends keeping fewer than 1,000 versions per blob.

Lifecycle management is the service-side mechanism for age-based cleanup. A policy can operate on current versions, previous versions, and snapshots. In the policy JSON, the `version` element refers to previous versions, while `baseBlob` refers to the current version. For this requirement, use a rule similar to this pattern:

```json
{
  "actions": {
    "version": {
      "delete": { "daysAfterCreationGreaterThan": 30 }
    }
  },
  "filters": {
    "blobTypes": [ "blockBlob" ]
  }
}
```

This targets historical versions after the age threshold. It does not delete the current blob unless you also configure a `baseBlob` delete action. Lifecycle policy changes can take time to go into effect, so this is not an instant cleanup button. If blob soft delete is enabled, a lifecycle delete can place objects into a soft-deleted state until retention expires.

Be cautious with tiering. Explicitly setting a tier on a version can change billing so that the full content length is charged in that tier, depending on the scenario.
